jit: a low P address is not proof of a fast interrupt

The fast-interrupt test was `_pc < Vba_End` alone — any address below the end of
the vector table. A fast-interrupt block deliberately does not advance the PC,
because the dispatcher restores it afterwards, so firmware that places ordinary
code in the unused tail of the vector table executes one instruction forever.
Require the processing mode as well.

--- a/source/dsp56kEmu/jitblock.cpp
+++ b/source/dsp56kEmu/jitblock.cpp
@@ -31,7 +31,41 @@ namespace dsp56k
 	{
 		const auto& opcodes = _dsp.opcodes();
 
-		const bool isFastInterrupt = _pc < Vba_End;
+		// ☠ A low P address is not proof of an interrupt. This used to be
+		// `_pc < Vba_End` alone, i.e. any address below 0x100, and a fast-interrupt
+		// block deliberately does NOT advance the PC because the interrupt dispatcher
+		// restores it afterwards. Firmware that places ORDINARY CODE in the unused
+		// tail of the vector table therefore executes one instruction forever.
+		//
+		// The Elektron Octatrack's DSP payload does exactly that: its entry point is
+		// P:0x0040 and its first module runs to P:0x025f, so every instruction of it
+		// was compiled as a fast interrupt. Observed as ~200 million executions of
+		// P:0x0040 with the accumulator correctly loaded and the PC never moving.
+		//
+		// ☠ And the processing mode is not the authority either, because this result is
+		// CACHED. An earlier version of this patch tested
+		// `_dsp.getProcessingMode() == DSP::FastInterrupt`, which is RUNTIME state,
+		// and baked the answer into a block the JIT keys on PC alone
+		// (DSP::m_jitEntries[pc], compiled once and reused for ever). Whichever mode
+		// a vector address happened to be compiled in FIRST therefore won permanently.
+		//
+		// When it lost, the damage was silent and fatal. JitOps::jsr emits
+		// setDspProcessingMode(LongInterrupt) ONLY when the block has a
+		// fast-interrupt mode (jitops.cpp:782-806), so a vector compiled as an
+		// ordinary block still did its pushPCSR() but never switched mode;
+		// DSP::execInterrupt then saw `m_processingMode != LongInterrupt`, restored
+		// the PC (dsp.cpp:233-238) and ORPHANED THE PUSHED FRAME. One leaked stack
+		// entry per host command, on a 16-deep hardware stack, until an rts returned
+		// to nothing, the PC reached 0 and the core executed the reset vector.
+		// Observed as core 0 retiring at P:0xfff000 with sp=0x0001.
+		//
+		// ✓ The authority is the ADDRESS, as upstream had it — upstream's boundary is
+		// simply wrong for this firmware. The Octatrack payload's vector table ends
+		// at P:0x3E (int_ESAI_TransmitLastSlot, the last vector of the ESAI group)
+		// and its ordinary code starts at P:0x40. So the table ends at 0x40, not at
+		// Vba_End's 0x100, and a pure address test is both correct AND deterministic
+		// under a PC-keyed cache.
+		const bool isFastInterrupt = _pc < g_vbaEnd;
 
 		const TWord pcMax = isFastInterrupt ? (_pc + 2) : _dsp.memory().sizeP();
 
@@ -236,7 +270,10 @@ namespace dsp56k
 
 		m_chain = _chain;
 
-		const bool isFastInterrupt = _pc < Vba_End;
+		// See the note in getInfo: the vector table ends at g_vbaEnd, not at
+		// Vba_End, and this test must stay a pure address comparison because the
+		// result is cached in a block the JIT keys on PC alone.
+		const bool isFastInterrupt = _pc < g_vbaEnd;
 		const auto fastInterruptMode = isFastInterrupt ? (m_config.dynamicFastInterrupts ? JitOps::FastInterruptMode::Dynamic : JitOps::FastInterruptMode::Static) : JitOps::FastInterruptMode::None;
 
 		dspAsm.clear();
diff --git a/source/dsp56kEmu/interrupts.h b/source/dsp56kEmu/interrupts.h
index d9f04d02..5f06e60a 100644
--- a/source/dsp56kEmu/interrupts.h
+++ b/source/dsp56kEmu/interrupts.h
@@ -30,6 +30,16 @@ namespace dsp56k
 		Vba_End										= 0x100,
 	};
 
+	// Where the vector table actually ends, which is not Vba_End. Vba_End is the
+	// architectural top of the 56300 vector space; real firmware stops short and
+	// puts ordinary code in the tail, and the JIT's "is this a fast interrupt"
+	// test is an address comparison -- a boundary too high compiles ordinary code
+	// as two-word fast-interrupt blocks that never advance the PC.
+	// The Octatrack payload's last vector is at P:0x3E and its entry point P:0x40.
+	// Keep this an ADDRESS: JitBlock caches by PC and compiles each block once,
+	// so anything runtime-dependent here is frozen on first compile.
+	static constexpr unsigned g_vbaEnd = 0x40;
+
 	enum InterruptVectorAddress56303
 	{
 		Vba_TIMER0compare							= 0x24,			// TIMER 0 compare								
